    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. one more thing, if the goods are actually lost or stolen from the container while at sea, what kind of documentation is usually required to prove to the insurance company that it wasn't just some simple clerical error? like what specific papers should we be looking for to back up the claim?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the case of lost or stolen goods during transit, there are several documents that could support the claim. These typically include the original bill of lading, which lists the shipping details and is crucial evidence that the goods were in fact transported by sea. Other required documents may involve an Inspection Certificate from the shipping line, showing the condition and count of goods upon loading; seaworthy documents if required, of the vessel; evidence of the contract of carriage, outlining the terms agreed upon by the buyer and seller; and proof that all port inspections were passed. Providing detailed and precise documentation will help establish a clear chain of custody of the goods and support the insurance claim.",
      "source": "target",
